  }

  [1mNo separate [38;5;153mremindAt[39m.[0m A task you care about is one with a due date, and the reminder fires when the due date arrives —
  so "overdue" and "reminder" are the same event. Adding a configurable lead time ("remind me 30 min before") is the
  obvious next feature, and leaving it out now costs nothing: it becomes a second field and a change to one pure
  function. All timestamps are ISO 8601 UTC, rendered in local time.

  [38;5;153mactiveReminders(state, now)[39m returns tasks where [38;5;153mdoneAt === null[39m, [38;5;153mdueAt !== null[39m, [38;5;153mnow >= dueAt[39m, and no live dismissal —
  sorted soonest-due first. The bell count is [38;5;153m.length[39m of that. That's the whole feature.

  [1mThe dismissal key is the one subtle rule.[0m It's [38;5;153mtaskId + ":" + dueAt[39m, not just [38;5;153mtaskId[39m. Change a task's due date and the
  key changes, so the old dismissal stops suppressing it and the rescheduled reminder fires properly — which is the
  behavior you want, and the thing a naive [38;5;153mtaskId[39m-keyed map gets wrong. Two consequences I'd make explicit in the spec:

  - [1mCompleting a task needs no dismissal[0m — [38;5;153mdoneAt[39m already excludes it. Reopening a still-overdue task brings its
    reminder back, which I think is correct: it's overdue again.
  - [1mDismissals need pruning[0m, or the map grows forever. On every save, drop keys whose task no longer exists or whose
    [38;5;153mdueAt[39m no longer matches. Cheap, and it keeps the store from accumulating garbage across edits and deletes.

  [1mToasts fire only on transition.[0m The scheduler holds an in-session set of keys it has already seen active; a reminder
  that becomes active while the page is open toasts once. Nothing toasts on page load — you'd get a barrage after a
  weekend. On load you just see the bell count, which is the honest representation of "these were waiting."
